{"entity": "Oscilloscope", "type": "artifact", "description": "An oscilloscope is an electronic test instrument used to display and measure the waveform parameters of electrical signals. It visualizes voltage as a function of time, enabling the observation and analysis of waveform shapes, frequency (calculated as 1/Δt), and peak-to-peak voltage. The instrument typically includes vertical, horizontal, and trigger systems, along with triggering, sampling, and automatic measurement functions. It features cursor measurement capabilities in manual, tracking, and automatic modes, and a MENU control area with a Cursor button for user interaction. The oscilloscope displays a channel settings menu, a trigger menu for configuring signal triggering, and an Acquire settings menu for configuring sampling methods. Conventional oscilloscopes require periodic signals to display stable waveforms.\n\nThe device is employed for waveform analysis in various contexts, such as observing and studying the waveforms of differentiating and integrating circuits, as well as Lissajous figures to measure phase differences. It is also used to monitor voltage waveforms at different points in filter circuits. A digital oscilloscope (e.g., Rigol brand) can display multiple waveform signals on a screen using four channels and includes mathematical operation capabilities. In practical setups, the oscilloscope may be connected to an amplifier output, labeled \"到示波器\" (to oscilloscope in Chinese), and is often used in conjunction with a function generator for signal analysis. Overall, the oscilloscope serves as a versatile electronic measurement device for analyzing electrical signal characteristics across a wide range of applications, depicted in schematic diagrams with various controls and input ports.<SEP>An instrument used to measure and visualize signal waveforms in electronic circuits.<SEP>An electronic instrument used to display and analyze waveform signals, specifically its menu interface schematic depicted in the image."}
